-# PicoMarQ 1.2.6 — validation and release status
+# PicoMarQ 1.2.7 — validation and release status
 
-**Baseline:** All modifications were made directly to the PicoMarQ 1.2.4 source and its supplied prebuilt UI, not 1.2.5. The source and bundled renderer keep the original 1.2.4 `ColorPicker` implementation.
+**Changes since 1.2.6** (`changes-since-1.2.6.patch`):
 
-**Changes:** JSON theme Save/Load via native dialogs, default portable `Themes` directory, fully validated appearance-only theme files, both light/dark color overrides, missing-font fallback to built-in Sans, and bold roman Heading 4 with its existing font size. Startup preferences, Smart Typography behavior, and all Markdown documents remain untouched by theme operations.
+- **Spelling of opened and pasted text, in US English.** Recent Chromium versions (from 143 or 144; Electron 44.6.0 has Chromium 152) spell-check only text they see typed into a text box the user clicked into: a privacy rule against websites probing the user's dictionary. PicoMarQ focuses its editor itself when a document opens and inserts pastes itself, so opened files and pasted text were never checked. `desktop/main.cjs` turns the rule off with the Chromium switch `UnrestrictSpellingAndGrammarForTesting` (PicoMarQ shows no websites) and sets the spelling language to `en-US`. After a change it did not see typed, Chromium rechecks only about 128 characters around the cursor, so after a paste or drop, and when returning from Markdown source view, `src/lib/spelling.ts` switches the editor's `spellcheck` attribute off and on, which makes Chromium recheck the whole document. Other underlines disappear until that recheck is done: about a second, 2.6 s in a 2 MB document.
+- **Settings > Size and Custom Width accept typed numbers** (`NumberBox` in `src/components/settings/EditorSettingsSection.tsx`). The old boxes forced every keystroke into range: the "1" of "18" became 12, then "128" became 24. A number in range now takes effect as it is typed; an out-of-range number moves to the nearest limit on Enter or on leaving the box; a box left empty shows the current value again. The arrow keys still step the value. Line Height already worked and is unchanged.
+- **Settings > Saved Themes > Restore Default Theme…** (`public/theme-presets-ui.js`, `restore_default_theme` in `desktop/main.cjs`, `defaultAppearance` in `desktop/theme-presets.cjs`). It asks first (Cancel is the default button). It then resets colors for Light and Dark, typography, and User Interface settings to the defaults a new profile starts with, the same values as the existing Reset buttons, and updates every open window the way Load Theme does. Light or Dark, Startup, Smart Typography, documents and theme files are not changed.
 
-**Tests performed:**
-- `node --test tests/*.test.cjs`: **28 passed** (including six new theme tests).
-- `node --experimental-strip-types --test tests/smart-typography.test.mts`: **5 passed**.
-- `python tests/colors-browser-smoke.py`: **passed** in Chromium, checking quotation rules, colors, themes and Markdown unchanged.
-- `python tests/theme-browser-smoke.py`: **passed** in Chromium, checking Heading 4 weight and size, appearance panel controls and invocations, source Markdown preservation and 1.2.4 picker operation.
-- All modified CommonJS and browser scripts pass Node syntax checking.
+**Build.** `npm ci` and `npm run build` (TypeScript, Vite) with Node 22.23.5 / npm 10.9.9. Before any change, the 1.2.6 source compiled to a JavaScript bundle byte-identical to the shipped 1.2.6 `index-N7AKkEmi.js`. 1.2.4–1.2.6 used a hand-patched stylesheet; 1.2.7 uses the stylesheet compiled from `src/App.css`. Every computed style of the torture-test document and of the whole Settings page, in Light and Dark (2,294 elements), is identical under the two stylesheets. Rebuilding this source package reproduces `dist/` byte for byte.
 
-**Limitations:** npm packages could not be downloaded (registry DNS failure); therefore a clean `npm ci && npm run build` / TypeScript build and the complete npm test suite could not be performed. This 1.2.6 release uses the prebuilt 1.2.4 renderer plus a shared standalone theme-settings UI script, rather than claiming to have recompiled the minified React bundle. Chromium UI tests do not exercise Electron's native Save/Open dialogs or Windows font enumeration. These require a native Windows test.
+**Tests** (Linux, Electron 44.6.0 official linux-x64 build with its published SHA-256, Xvfb):
 
-**Windows portable distribution:** Assembled offline from the **supplied PicoMarQ Windows x64 1.2.4 runtime**, replacing resources/app/desktop and resources/app/dist with this source release. The unsigned executable's FileVersion and ProductVersion resources were changed from 1.2.4 to 1.2.6, without changing executable code sections or Electron dependencies. ZIP integrity and source/resource equivalence are checked. This is offline packaging, not a rebuilt Electron executable.
+- `npm test`: 39 passed (29 + 10), including new Restore Default Theme tests.
+- `tests/theme-browser-smoke.py` (updated for the third button) and `tests/colors-browser-smoke.py`: passed in Chrome for Testing 153.
+- `tests/desktop-smoke.cjs` (18 checks), `typography-smoke.cjs`, `startup-smoke.cjs`, `close-restore-smoke.cjs`, `close-command-smoke.cjs`: passed. The typography test still expected the pre-1.2.6 italic Heading 4 and failed the same way on 1.2.6; it now expects the bold Heading 4.
+- New `tests/settings-smoke.cjs`: typing in Size and Custom Width; Restore Default Theme with Cancel, then Restore.
+- New `tests/spelling-smoke.cjs`: 24 misspellings (including the British "colour" and "organise") in an opened file, one- and three-paragraph pastes, typing, a source-view edit, and a ten-paragraph paste. All are underlined within about 1.2 s, with no underline under any correctly spelled word. The same test fails on 1.2.6 (none of the opened file's misspellings underlined, even with US English set). The dictionary is Ubuntu's US English Hunspell (`hunspell-en-us`, SCOWL 2020.12.07), converted by `scripts/hunspell-to-bdic.py`, a port of Chromium's converter. Loaded into Electron, it accepts all 78,990 of its words and marks British spellings and common typos.
+- Large documents (2 MB, 4,400 paragraphs): typing latency to the next frame is a median of 34 ms (33 ms in 1.2.6). The whole document is checked within 7 s of opening. After a short paste, the paragraph is underlined in 0.2 s and the whole document is rechecked in 2.6 s, against 1.2 s for 100 KB.
+- The packaged `resources/app` from the Windows ZIP passed the same spelling, settings, and Restore checks.
+
+**Windows portable distribution.** Assembled by `scripts/repack-windows-from-1.2.6.py` from the 1.2.6 portable release. Its Electron 44.6.0 runtime is unchanged; `resources/app` (desktop, dist, package.json) and the text files are replaced. Only four version-resource fields of `PicoMarQ.exe` change, from 1.2.6 to 1.2.7, as checked independently with `pefile`: every section except `.rsrc` is identical, 4 bytes of `.rsrc` differ, and the entry point is the same.
+Baseline executable SHA-256: `23f746841b54f40d90f7e0ed019232d664d4c321080e627af89774060d159713`
+1.2.7 executable SHA-256: `914236887c47e0583bdb6acd394c4159dfacab023baf5229a1ee11b5f8056ccb`
+
+**Limitations.** Not run on Windows. On Windows, Electron may use the Windows spell checker instead of Hunspell, but the rule and the recheck are in Chromium's page engine, so the behavior should be the same; confirm with a file that has a typo. The switch's name ends in "ForTesting". It exists in Electron 44.6.0 (Chromium 152), and if a later Electron drops it, `tests/spelling-smoke.cjs` fails. The drag-and-drop recheck uses the same code as paste but is not exercised by a test.
